    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/elias-lonnrot"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elias-lonnrot.jpg" alt="Elias Lönnrot" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/elias-lonnrot">Elias Lönnrot</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elias_L%C3%B6nnrot_portrait-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Elias_Lönnrot_potrait.jpg: unknwon derivative work: Bff, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> had been ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> for more than five centuries before it was ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1809 and became the autonomous Grand Duchy of Finland, with its own Senate, Diet, laws and, from 1860, its own currency, the markka. In the nineteenth century a national movement promoted the Finnish language and culture, inspired by the epic <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalevala">Kalevala</a></em>, compiled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elias-lonnrot">Elias Lönnrot</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russification">Russification</a> policies from 1899 provoked resistance. The parliamentary reform of 1906 created a single-chamber parliament, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-finland">Eduskunta</a>, elected by universal suffrage, the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to give women both the vote and the right to stand. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution of 1917</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a> moved towards separation, and on 6 December 1917 parliament approved the declaration of independence presented by the Senate under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pehr-evind-svinhufvud">Pehr Evind Svinhufvud</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Lenin</a>’s government recognised it on the last day of the year. Independence was immediately followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finnish-civil-war">Civil War of January to May 1918</a> between the socialist Reds, based in the industrial south, and the government’s Whites under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-gustaf-emil-mannerheim">Carl Gustaf Emil Mannerheim</a>, who won with German help. A German prince, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-frederick-charles-of-hesse">Frederick Charles of Hesse</a>, was elected king in October 1918 but renounced the throne after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>’s defeat, and the constitution of July 1919 made Finland a republic with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/k-j-stahlberg">K. J. Ståhlberg</a> as its first president.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sami-people"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sami-people.jpg" alt="Sámi people" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sami-people">Sámi people</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sami_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jeltz, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> began as a poor, largely agrarian country, in which most people lived from farming and forestry. The land reforms of 1918 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lex-kallio">Lex Kallio</a> of 1922 enabled tenant farmers and the landless to acquire their own farms, creating a broad class of smallholders that stabilised society after the Civil War. Exports were dominated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, pulp and paper, earning the forest industry the nickname green gold. Finnish and Swedish were both official languages, and the Swedish-speaking minority, once the country’s elite, kept its own schools and institutions; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sami-people">Sámi</a> of the north remained a small indigenous minority. The Evangelical Lutheran Church was the church of the great majority. After 1945, war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, paid in ships, machinery and cables, stimulated engineering and metal industries, and bilateral trade with the Soviet Union became a major outlet for Finnish goods. Rapid urbanisation in the 1960s and 1970s saw many people move to the south and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. A Nordic welfare state was built, with free comprehensive schooling from the 1970s that later earned international attention through high PISA results. In the 1990s the electronics firm <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nokia">Nokia</a> became the world’s largest maker of mobile phones, and information technology joined forestry and engineering as pillars of a high-income economy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-suomussalmi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-suomussalmi.jpg" alt="Battle of Suomussalmi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-suomussalmi">Battle of Suomussalmi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_suomussalmi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Edward J. Krasnobowski, Frank Martini, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Finnish army grew out of the White forces of the Civil War, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/27th-jager-battalion">Jäger battalion</a> of volunteers trained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and it was built on universal male conscription with a large trained reserve. In the 1920s and 1930s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> fortified the Karelian Isthmus with the defensive line later named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-gustaf-emil-mannerheim">Mannerheim</a>. When Finland refused Soviet demands for territory, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> attacked on 30 November 1939, starting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war">Winter War</a>. Outnumbered Finnish troops, fighting on skis in forests at temperatures far below freezing, destroyed Soviet divisions at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-suomussalmi">Suomussalmi</a> and held the isthmus until February 1940. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow-peace-treaty">Moscow Peace Treaty</a> of March 1940 ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Viipuri</a> and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karelia">Karelia</a>, about a tenth of the country, from which over 400,000 people were evacuated. Finland joined the German attack on the Soviet Union in June 1941 and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continuation-war">Continuation War</a>, recapturing and advancing beyond the lost lands. The Soviet offensive of June 1944 was stopped at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tali-ihantala">Tali-Ihantala</a>, and the armistice of September 1944 restored the 1940 border, added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petsamo">Petsamo</a> to the losses and obliged Finland to expel German troops in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lapland-war">Lapland War</a> of 1944–1945. About 90,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a> died in these wars. Finland later maintained a large reserve army and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in 2023.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/juho-kusti-paasikivi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juho-kusti-paasikivi.jpg" alt="Juho Kusti Paasikivi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/juho-kusti-paasikivi">Juho Kusti Paasikivi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:J.K._Paasikivi_1945_portrait_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">F.E Fremling, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The constitution of 1919 created a parliamentary republic with a strong president, who directed foreign policy, appointed governments and could dissolve parliament. The early republic faced threats from both sides: the Communist Party was banned until 1944, and the anti-communist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lapua-movement">Lapua Movement</a>, which kidnapped opponents, ended after the failed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mantsala-rebellion">Mäntsälä rebellion</a> of 1932. Democracy nevertheless survived the interwar period, unlike in most new states of the region, and the Social Democrats returned to government in 1937. The Treaty of Tartu of 1920 fixed the border with Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petsamo">Petsamo</a>, and in 1921 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> confirmed Finnish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over the Åland Islands, which received broad autonomy. After 1944 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juho-kusti-paasikivi">Juho Kusti Paasikivi</a> and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urho-kekkonen">Urho Kekkonen</a>, president from 1956 to 1982, pursued neutrality combined with careful friendship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> under the treaty of 1948, a policy critics abroad called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finlandisation">Finlandisation</a>; Kekkonen’s long rule concentrated great power in the presidency. Proportional representation produced coalition governments, often led by the Social Democrats, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/centre-party-finland">Centre Party</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-coalition-party">National Coalition Party</a>. The constitution of 2000 shifted power from the president to parliament and the prime minister, although the president still leads foreign policy together with the government.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/moscow"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moscow.jpg" alt="Moscow" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Basil%27s_Cathedral_and_the_Red_Square.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">U.S. Department of State, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>’s survival as an independent democracy through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, though fighting on the losing side, was unusual among the small states bordering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and its policy of neutrality and accommodation with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> became a much-discussed model of how a small state could deal with a superpower. Helsinki hosted the Olympic Games of 1952 and the Conference on Security and Co-operation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsinki-final-act">Helsinki Final Act</a> of 1975 set principles on borders and human rights that later helped dissidents across the Eastern bloc. Finnish diplomats and soldiers took part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> peacekeeping, and former president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martti-ahtisaari">Martti Ahtisaari</a> received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 2008 for his mediation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>. Finnish design and architecture gained worldwide recognition through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvar-aalto">Alvar Aalto</a> and firms such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marimekko">Marimekko</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iittala">Iittala</a>, and the composer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-sibelius">Jean Sibelius</a> became a national symbol. The country’s comprehensive schools, gender equality, low corruption and high trust rankings made it a frequent reference point in international comparisons. In technology, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nokia">Nokia</a>’s mobile phones and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linux">Linux</a> operating system, created by the Helsinki student <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linus-torvalds">Linus Torvalds</a> in 1991, had a global reach far out of proportion to a population of about five and a half million.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tarja-halonen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tarja-halonen.jpg" alt="Tarja Halonen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tarja-halonen">Tarja Halonen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tarja_Halonen_1c389_8827-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tarja_Halonen_1c389_8827.jpg: Janwikifoto derivative work: Bff (talk), CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> still exists, and its modern history is one of transformation rather than decline. The collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1991 ended the trade on which much Finnish industry depended and, together with a banking crisis, caused a deep depression in which output fell by more than a tenth and unemployment rose above 15 per cent. Recovery came through devaluation, restructuring and the rise of the electronics industry. Freed from Soviet constraints, Finland applied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-communities">European Communities</a> and, after a referendum in 1994, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> on 1 January 1995; it adopted the euro in 1999, unlike its Nordic neighbours. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarja-halonen">Tarja Halonen</a> became the first female president in 2000. After 2008 the global financial crisis, the decline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nokia">Nokia</a>’s phone business and problems in the paper industry brought a long period of slow growth. Finland remained militarily non-aligned until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>’s full-scale invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> in February 2022, after which public opinion shifted sharply, and it joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> on 4 April 2023, ending more than seven decades of neutrality and non-alignment. Russia responded with hostility, and Finland closed its eastern border crossings in late 2023. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-stubb">Alexander Stubb</a> succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sauli-niinisto">Sauli Niinistö</a> as president in March 2024. Today Finland is a wealthy parliamentary democracy within the European Union and NATO.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> is a small Nordic state that won independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1917 and kept it through a civil war and two wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, then built a prosperous welfare democracy and moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> neutrality into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>.</em></p>

